  "asr_text": "The key point is that, at the time of that report, the tariff-driven rise had not yet fully made its way through the supply chain to the prices consumers paid. The moment the cost of the beans goes up, the store price tag does not jump up and say, got it! Price rises take time to show up. In other words, a price tag is not a live news broadcast. It is a letter that arrives a little late. Even if you open it today, it may not be about something that happened today. It may tell you about an earlier drought or bean shortage or high prices in the market. If you look only at the latest headline, you may get the sender wrong. Next time you look twice at a price tag in the store, try adding, when did this happen, to why is it expensive? Coffee may be bitter, but we want our detective work to be clear. Before that first cup, turn back the hands of the clock a little.",
